WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.101 --> 00:00:01.811
(audience applauding)
(mellow music)

00:00:01.811 --> 00:00:02.790
- I'm not kidding. I know.

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:05.820
I could quote so many "Frasier"
episodes for you right now.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.944
Whenever I found out they were
rebooting it, I freaked out.

00:00:08.944 --> 00:00:09.777
- Did you?

00:00:09.777 --> 00:00:10.890
Wow. (chuckles)
- Yes, I'm not kidding.

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:11.723
- Thank you. (chuckles)

00:00:11.723 --> 00:00:14.910
- The level of wit, the comedic timing,

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:17.730
the physical comedy, all
of it, it's all there.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:19.830
And I think it's one of those...

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.240
Not to dog any shows now,

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.310
I just think there aren't
really shows like that now,

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.390
so I was so excited.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.620
Why did you wanna reboot it now?

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:26.652
- Thanks.

00:00:26.652 --> 00:00:27.990
Well, it seemed like
the right time to do it.

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.980
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.220
- Six years ago, the "Roseanne"
show kinda came back.

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.460
- Yeah.
- And then it was a big,

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:34.293
big hit.

00:00:34.293 --> 00:00:35.126
- Yeah.
- And I thought,

00:00:35.126 --> 00:00:37.350
well, you know, "Frasier"
hasn't gone anywhere.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.150
He's just sort of drifted off into the-

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:39.983
- He is a part

00:00:39.983 --> 00:00:40.816
of our family.
- The mist.

00:00:40.816 --> 00:00:41.777
- Yes.
- Right.

00:00:41.777 --> 00:00:43.620
- I love him.
- And it seemed

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:44.970
like a good idea to bring him back

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:45.840
'cause he was always funny.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.220
- Yeah.
- And he had a nice take

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:48.540
on the world, and he loved the world,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.920
and he wanted to make things better,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.540
so it seemed like it was a good time

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:53.640
to step back into the role.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:54.473
- Yeah.

00:00:54.473 --> 00:00:55.306
Did you...

00:00:55.306 --> 00:00:57.450
'Cause your voice, I mean,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:00.450
just one of the greatest voices,
speaking voice, singing...

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.668
It's just a great tone.
- Oh, thank you.

00:01:02.668 --> 00:01:03.656
For me, that's a big compliment.

00:01:03.656 --> 00:01:05.460
Thank you.
- Well, it's a gorgeous tone

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.200
that people like dream of.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.700
And did you always...

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.110
Did you always think you
were gonna do the theme song

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:10.943
to "Frasier"?

00:01:10.943 --> 00:01:11.776
Was there ever any other idea?

00:01:11.776 --> 00:01:13.560
- Well, what I did was, I said to them,

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.690
the producers at the time,
I said, "Oh, by the way,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.730
I am gonna sing the theme song."

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.729
- Is that what you did?
(audience chuckles)

00:01:18.729 --> 00:01:19.927
You're just like, "Oh."
- Yeah, and they said,

00:01:19.927 --> 00:01:21.960
"Well, we're not planning
on making a theme song."

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.752
I said, "Well, maybe we
should plan on making one."

00:01:23.752 --> 00:01:24.585
(audience laughing)

00:01:24.585 --> 00:01:25.770
- I love it. I love it.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:26.603
You're just like, "No, no,

00:01:26.603 --> 00:01:27.436
I have this (indistinct)."
- So we ended up

00:01:27.436 --> 00:01:28.710
with this sort of weird hybrid song.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.540
It doesn't have like a middle
verse or anything like that.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.373
It doesn't have like-
- Yeah.

00:01:31.373 --> 00:01:32.610
- A whole story to tell,

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.830
but it is that sort of jazzy sting thing,

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.870
which seemed really
appropriate, and it has

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.250
become very popular.
- It's so appropriate

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.230
for Frasier Crane.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:43.260
It fits so perfectly and it's
in everyone's heads forever.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.572
I love it so much.

00:01:44.572 --> 00:01:45.405
- Thank you.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:46.290
- Thank you for singing it with me.

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.920
I was very excited.
- Oh, it's my honor, honestly.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:48.990
- And I just love-
- Thank you.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:49.980
- That jazz vibe,

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:50.937
like that whole setup-
- Yeah.

00:01:50.937 --> 00:01:51.770
- Is so fun to sing.
- Well, we changed it

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:53.460
a little bit for this incarnation

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:54.296
of the show-
- Yeah.

00:01:54.296 --> 00:01:55.890
- Because I thought,
well, he's a little wiser,

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:56.730
he's a little more mellow,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:58.230
he's more comfortable in his own skin.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.063
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:59.063 --> 00:02:00.840
- And so we should like lower it

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.030
and kinda ease into it a little bit more.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:03.863
- I love that.

00:02:03.863 --> 00:02:04.800
So that was purposeful-

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:05.633
- Yeah, absolutely.
- The new arrangement

00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:06.466
for new "Frasier."

00:02:06.466 --> 00:02:07.890
- Yeah.
- Yeah, I love that.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:08.820
I hear that you always have...

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:09.653
You must love singing.

00:02:09.653 --> 00:02:11.820
You always have karaoke at your parties?

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.190
- Well, I didn't use to
have karaoke at my parties

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.050
until I met my wife.
- Why?

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.565
So she's better. (laughs)
- She's the karaoke genius.

00:02:18.565 --> 00:02:19.590
- So I love her.
- She actually is a genius

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.423
at it.

00:02:20.423 --> 00:02:21.256
- Okay.
- I mean, it's amazing.

00:02:21.256 --> 00:02:22.980
- What's a go-to song
for either one of you?

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.470
- Well, she sings Karen Carpenter

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:27.557
like Karen Carpenter couldn't.
(Kelly gasps)

00:02:27.557 --> 00:02:28.590
- Oh.
- I mean, it's amazing.

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.440
It's clear as bell.
- She's here.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:30.360
- Yeah, she's here.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:31.193
She's up there.

00:02:31.193 --> 00:02:32.026
- Oh, my gosh.
(audience chuckling)

00:02:32.026 --> 00:02:33.030
I love Karen Carpenter.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:33.900
- [Kelsey] It's amazing.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.733
- Yeah.
- I mean,

00:02:34.733 --> 00:02:35.587
when she sings those songs,

00:02:35.587 --> 00:02:37.680
"I can take all the madness
the world has to give,"

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:38.521
oh, my God.

00:02:38.521 --> 00:02:39.360
- [Kelly] Ah.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.412
- It's just, it's extraordinary,
and it's so clear.

00:02:41.412 --> 00:02:42.245
- And an intense life, yeah.
- And she also sings

00:02:42.245 --> 00:02:43.141
"Desperado" like

00:02:43.141 --> 00:02:43.974
(Kelly gasps)

00:02:43.974 --> 00:02:45.900
no one's ever sung it.
- I just sang "Desperado"

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:46.740
here, ma'am.
(audience laughing)

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.000
- It's about the sexiest thing

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.833
you've ever heard in your life.

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:49.666
- [Kelly] Oh, my God.

00:02:49.666 --> 00:02:51.930
- When she says, "Open the
gate," it's like, oh. (chuckles)

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:52.800
- Yeah.
(audience laughing)

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:53.633
- Baby.
- You're like,

00:02:53.633 --> 00:02:54.714
"Fine, I will."
(Kelsey laughs)

00:02:54.714 --> 00:02:55.547
- Come on.
- (chuckles) You're like,

00:02:55.547 --> 00:02:56.610
"I love it."
- Come on in.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.530
- Wait, what's your go-to?

00:02:58.530 --> 00:02:59.820
- I tend to sing...

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.518
I'll do "Let's Dance" by David Bowie.

00:03:01.518 --> 00:03:02.351
(Kelly gasps)

00:03:02.351 --> 00:03:04.020
And we have another
distinction that I share

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.880
with David Bowie, which
is very interesting.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:09.270
I had a little heart
trouble about 15 years ago.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:10.103
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:10.103 --> 00:03:10.936
- And what the nurses
told me was they said,

00:03:10.936 --> 00:03:12.548
"Oh, David Bowie was in
this bed before you."

00:03:12.548 --> 00:03:13.950
(Kelly gasps)
(audience laughing)

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.602
- You're like, "I'm gonna make it."

00:03:15.602 --> 00:03:17.040
- I'm like, "Finally,
finally, I've arrived."

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.290
- That's amazing.
(audience laughing)

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.123
- And then-

00:03:20.123 --> 00:03:21.120
- What a random-
- And the guy in before him

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:22.350
was Paul Newman.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.183
So I was like-

00:03:23.183 --> 00:03:24.180
- Are are you serious?
- Obviously, they're probably

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.013
not supposed to tell you,

00:03:25.013 --> 00:03:27.240
but that particular-
- Does this person only work

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.590
on super famous people?
(audience laughing)

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:29.897
I was like, weird.
- I think that

00:03:29.897 --> 00:03:31.560
it was that room, apparently,

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.240
so it's pretty great.
- Oh, my gosh.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.292
- Yeah.
- But back to "Frasier."

00:03:34.292 --> 00:03:35.400
- Back to "Frasier."

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.540
- Because I'm very excited.
(audience laughing)

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.010
So back in Boston.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:38.843
- Yes.

00:03:38.843 --> 00:03:39.676
- [Kelly] So this was all purposeful.

00:03:39.676 --> 00:03:40.619
- Well-
- This is like full circle

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:42.420
from Tuesdays, yeah.
- At first, I wanted to...

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.100
I wanted to bring the
whole legacy cast back

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.380
but in a different world
where we all moved on

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:47.213
to something else-

00:03:47.213 --> 00:03:48.046
- Yeah.
- But we would still

00:03:48.046 --> 00:03:48.960
be connected.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:49.980
That didn't work out.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:50.813
- Okay.

00:03:50.813 --> 00:03:52.620
- So once that happened,
after about three years

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:56.250
of really trying to kinda
sell that idea to everybody,

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.230
I went home and thought,
well, what do we do now?

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.097
And I actually, in a dream, I said,

00:04:00.097 --> 00:04:01.950
"Oh, he's gotta go back to Boston.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.270
He hasn't finished.

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.800
He didn't leave Boston the right way."

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:05.633
- Yeah.

00:04:05.633 --> 00:04:06.810
- The original concept was that he had

00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:08.400
to get all the way across the country

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.200
'cause we just wanted
to get away from Boston

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.520
to start the new show.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:12.353
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:12.353 --> 00:04:15.000
- But that was just a kind of
a geographical happenstance.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.420
I thought, in this one, he left,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.160
the thing with Diane didn't work out,

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:21.570
Lilith and he got divorced,

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:23.010
his son has been in Boston ever since.

00:04:23.010 --> 00:04:23.982
I thought, he's got stuff-

00:04:23.982 --> 00:04:24.815
- Freddy.
- He's gotta do

00:04:24.815 --> 00:04:25.648
in Boston still.

00:04:25.648 --> 00:04:26.820
- Yeah.
- And so when he went there

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.030
as a young man to first go to Harvard,

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:32.559
it didn't work out the
way he wanted it to.

00:04:32.559 --> 00:04:33.392
And so he had some stuff-
- That's beautiful.

00:04:33.392 --> 00:04:34.346
- To deal with, yeah.

00:04:34.346 --> 00:04:35.179
- Yeah.

00:04:35.179 --> 00:04:36.450
- So we're back in Boston.
- And so relatable.

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:37.283
- [Kelsey] Yeah.

00:04:37.283 --> 00:04:39.210
- I love that there's a
tribute to John Mahoney,

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.440
who played your father.
- Yeah.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.910
- Is that the name of the bar?

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:42.870
- Mahoney's Bar, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:44.070
- [Kelsey] The baby's name is John.

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:44.903
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:45.758 --> 00:04:46.591
- The son...
- Ah.

00:04:46.591 --> 00:04:47.850
- Daphne and Niles'-
- Ah.

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:48.813
- Ah, loved him.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.680
- And Eddie, ah.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:53.918
Yeah, you have no idea.
- Yeah. (chuckles)

00:04:53.918 --> 00:04:54.900
- I have...

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.750
- But yeah, John died a few years back,

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:01.080
and I just thought, well,
we can't do the show again

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:02.130
without honoring John.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.540
We just can't.
- Yeah.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:07.140
- And it has to begin with
the fact that John is gone

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.010
and Dad's gone,

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:09.141
Martin's gone.
- Yeah.

00:05:09.141 --> 00:05:11.790
- And so then I become Dad, you know?

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.830
- Oh, my gosh.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.988
Okay, my makeup artist will kill me.

00:05:15.988 --> 00:05:17.070
(audience laughing)

00:05:17.070 --> 00:05:18.600
I'm a contagious crier,

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.433
so I'm like...
- Yeah, me too.

00:05:19.433 --> 00:05:20.266
(all laughing)

00:05:20.266 --> 00:05:21.099
- I'm gonna be fine. (sniffles)

00:05:21.099 --> 00:05:22.410
Just yeah, okay.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.900
In Boston, though, you're back there,

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:27.300
so do you think any "Cheers"
characters will come in

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:28.133
and out, do you think?
- No, no.

00:05:28.133 --> 00:05:28.966
- [Kelly] No?

00:05:28.966 --> 00:05:29.820
- [Kelsey] I mean, look-

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.235
- Don't let me down, "Cheers" people!

00:05:31.235 --> 00:05:32.670
(audience laughing)

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.050
I was like, that would be amazing.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:35.670
Just one.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:37.015
- The bar is closed.

00:05:37.015 --> 00:05:37.848
- [Kelly] Ah.

00:05:37.848 --> 00:05:40.500
- I mean, Jimmy's decided that
the bar went out of business.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:41.333
- [Kelly] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:41.333 --> 00:05:42.180
- For whatever reason.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:44.460
But I would like to see Diane come back.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:45.293
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:45.293 --> 00:05:47.970
- Just for one of those
reasons of Frasier being able

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:49.890
to put something to bed,

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.420
to end up with a nice thing-
- Closure.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:52.253
- Between them, yeah.

00:05:52.253 --> 00:05:53.580
- Yeah.

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.560
- And to kind of rediscover how much...

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.080
'Cause when I first got the role,

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:01.020
the key to Frasier for
me was that he loved her

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.500
with his whole heart, that he
loved with all of his being.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:05.970
And that actually defined who he is.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.260
He's been that way ever since.

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:08.093
- Yeah.

00:06:08.093 --> 00:06:09.420
- He just goes in
wholeheartedly with everything

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:10.800
and that's what makes him funny.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:12.510
- Yeah, so witty.

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:13.343
Oh, my gosh.

00:06:13.343 --> 00:06:15.390
God, I'm just so excited
(audience cheering)

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:16.260
the show is back on.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.560
The level of wit is ah, and the elitism

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:20.700
is just so funny.
- It's still there,

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:21.840
yeah, yeah.
- Oh, my God, I love it,

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:22.890
still there.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:23.760
I have to say this, too.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.050
Speaking of your wife we
were talking about earlier,

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:27.120
y'all, you're celebrating
your anniversary soon, right?

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:28.020
- February 25th, yeah.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:29.730
- [Kelsey] And you were
married in New York City.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.610
- We were married on the
stage of the Longacre Theatre

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.477
where I was performing
"La Cage aux Folles."

00:06:35.477 --> 00:06:36.460
- [Kelly] What?

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:38.843
- So Kayte and I met when I
rehearsed that, actually, and-

00:06:38.843 --> 00:06:40.950
- Ah, that's magical.
- That's when we fell in love.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:41.970
Well, the show is about love.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:42.803
We fell in love.

00:06:42.803 --> 00:06:44.310
It just was a perfect combination.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:45.443
- Ah.
- Then we had a-

00:06:45.443 --> 00:06:46.426
- What a movie.

00:06:46.426 --> 00:06:47.520
- We had the ceremony on the stage,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:50.010
and then we had our
reception at The Plaza.

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:51.540
And people still talk about that.

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:52.560
It was really fun.
